## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical thinking for real-time services, focusing on WebSocket handshakes, pub/sub latency, and backpressure—common beginner pitfalls when scaling.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket handshake overhead
- Publish/subscribe latency
- Backpressure and queue depth
- Simple workload ramping
- Component isolation for bottleneck diagnosis

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton k6 WebSocket test
import ws from 'k6/ws';
import { check } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  const res = ws.connect('wss://chat.example.test/ws', {}, function (socket) {
    socket.on('open', function () {
      socket.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'msg', text: 'hello' }));
    });
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you determine if latency is in handshake vs Redis vs DB?
- What would your SLO look like for 99th percentile latency under peak load?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-time presence is sensitive to tail latency and backpressure. This question probes how an interviewer evaluates scalable WebSocket gateways, heartbeat resilience, and autoscaling behavior under bursty traffic, mirroring production issues at large-scale messaging apps.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket scale and connection churn
- Backpressure propagation across gateway and bus
- Heartbeat/timestamp accuracy and liveness tuning
- Autoscaling strategies (Kubernetes/HPA, event-driven scaling)
- Test harnesses: k6/ws, Gatling, Locust

## Code Example
```
import ws from 'k6/ws';
export const options = {vus: 1000, duration: '2m'};
export default function () {
  const url = 'wss://presence.example';
  ws.connect(url, {}, function (socket) {
    socket.on('open', function () {
      socket.send(JSON.stringify({type:'presence', status:'online'}));
    });
